        <!-- SECTION 1: WHAT IS DIGITAL FORENSICS -->
        <section id="introduction">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 01</span>
            <h2>The Digital Detective</h2>
            <div class="intro-lead">
                Every time you touch a digital device, you leave a trail. Digital forensics is the art of following that
                trail.
            </div>

            <p>Imagine you are a detective in the 21st century. Instead of just looking for physical fingerprints or
                dropped items at a crime scene, you are looking for "digital dust." This dust is everywhere: in your
                browser history, your hidden files, and even in the data you thought you deleted years ago.</p>

            <p><strong>Digital Forensics</strong> is a branch of forensic science that focuses on identifying,
                preserving, recovering, analyzing, and presenting data stored on digital devices. Whether it's a
                smartphone, a laptop, a smart fridge, or a massive server in the cloud, if it stores data, it can be
                investigated.</p>

            <div class="definition">
                <h3>The Official Definition</h3>
                <p>"The application of scientifically proven methods toward the preservation, collection, validation,
                    identification, analysis, interpretation, documentation, and presentation of digital evidence
                    derived from digital sources."</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Why do we need it?</h3>
            <p>In the past, most crimes were solved with physical evidence. Today, almost every crime has a digital
                component. A person accused of a physical crime might have their location tracked by their phone's GPS.
                A corporate thief might have their emails recovered even after hitting 'empty trash'.</p>

            <div class="feature-grid">
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <span class="icon-box">⚖️</span>
                    <h4>Criminal Justice</h4>
                    <p>Solving murders, fraud, and drug trafficking by finding messages and photos suspect's thought
                        were gone.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <span class="icon-box">🏢</span>
                    <h4>Corporate Integrity</h4>
                    <p>Catching employees who try to steal company secrets or "trade secrets" to sell to competitors.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <span class="icon-box">🛡️</span>
                    <h4>Cyber Defense</h4>
                    <p>Finding out how a hacker got into a system and what exactly they stole during a data breach.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p>But here is the catch: digital evidence is incredibly fragile. One wrong click can change the "last
                accessed" date of a file. Pulling the power cord on a running computer can destroy the data currently
                sitting in its "thinking" memory (RAM). This is why digital forensics follows very strict scientific
                rules.</p>

            <p>In the modern era, our digital footprints are more revealing than our physical ones. A single smartphone
                can contain over 50,000 photos, thousands of messages, and a minute-by-minute log of where the user has
                been for the last three years. This makes digital forensics one of the most powerful tools in any
                investigator's arsenal.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 2: HOW IT WORKS (THE SCIENCE) -->
        <section id="how-it-works">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 02</span>
            <h2>The Science of Memory</h2>
            <p>To understand forensics, you first need to understand how computers actually store information. Think of
                a computer's storage (like a Hard Drive or SSD) like a giant library. When you "save" a file, the
                librarian writes it in a book and puts it on a shelf.</p>

            <h3>The Secret of Deletion</h3>
            <p>When you "delete" a file and empty the recycle bin, the librarian doesn't actually burn the book. They
                simply go to the index at the front of the library and erase the line that says where that book is. The
                book stays on the shelf until the librarian needs more space and writes a NEW book over it.</p>

            <div class="pro-tip">
                <strong>Forensic Fact:</strong> Professional forensic investigators can recover "deleted" files months
                or even years later, as long as that specific part of the drive hasn't been used to store new
                information!
            </div>

            <p>This concept of "unallocated space" is where the most juicy evidence is often found. When a criminal
                tries to hide their tracks by deleting files, they are actually just giving the investigator a puzzle to
                solve. Forensic software can scan the entire drive, looking for file "headers" that indicate where a
                photo or a document begins, even if it's no longer in the computer's index.</p>

            <h3>Bits and Bytes: The Language of Truth</h3>
            <p>Computers only speak in 1s and 0s. A forensic investigator doesn't just look at what the computer "shows"
                on the screen. They look at the raw data underneath. They use a method called <strong>"Hexadecimal
                    View"</strong> to see the absolute truth of what is on a disk.</p>

            <p>This is important because criminals often try to hide files by changing their names. For example, a
                criminal might change a photo of a secret document named <code>stolen_data.jpg</code> to
                <code>homework.txt</code>. A normal person would try to open it as a text file and see garbage. A
                forensic tool looks at the "header" (the very first few bits of the file) and says: "Wait, this says
                it's a JPEG photo, even though the name says TXT!"</p>

            <p>Every file type has a unique "signature" or "magic number." For example, every PDF file starts with the
                characters `%PDF`. Every JPEG image starts with `FF D8 FF`. No matter what you rename the file to, these
                bytes stay the same. This is why it's almost impossible to hide digital evidence from a trained
                professional.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 3: BRANCHES OF FORENSICS -->
        <section id="branches">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 03</span>
            <h2>The Many Faces of Forensics</h2>
            <p>Digital forensics is a huge field. Just like you have different doctors for your heart, your teeth, and
                your eyes, there are different forensic specialists for different types of technology.</p>

            <div class="feature-grid">
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>💻 Computer Forensics</h4>
                    <p>The classic branch. It involves looking at desktops and laptops. Experts look for browser
                        history, hidden folders, and registry keys that show what USB drives were plugged into the
                        machine.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>📱 Mobile Forensics</h4>
                    <p>This is the busiest field today. Phones are basically small computers that track our every move.
                        Investigators extract text messages, WhatsApp chats, GPS locations, and even deleted photos from
                        Instagram.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>🌐 Network Forensics</h4>
                    <p>This is about "data in motion." Experts look at the internet traffic going in and out of a
                        building. They can see what websites a person visited and what files they uploaded to a hacker's
                        server.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>☁️ Cloud Forensics</h4>
                    <p>Since more people use Google Drive, Dropbox, and iCloud, investigators now have to get data from
                        servers owned by big companies like Amazon and Microsoft.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p>There are also newer branches like **IoT Forensics** (Internet of Things). Did you know that a smart
                doorbell or a smart toaster can sometimes provide the evidence needed to solve a crime? If a person
                claims they weren't at home at 2:00 AM, but their smart coffee maker started brewing at that exact time,
                the digital evidence proves they were lying!</p>

            <p>Another fascinating area is **Database Forensics**. Large companies store their most valuable information
                in databases. When a "leak" happens, database forensic experts can look at the logs to see exactly which
                employee ran a search for "customer credit card numbers" at 3:00 AM on a Sunday.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 4: CHAIN OF CUSTODY -->
        <section id="custody">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 04</span>
            <h2>The Chain of Custody</h2>
            <p>This is the most important part of any investigation. If you find the perfect evidence but you don't
                follow the <strong>Chain of Custody</strong>, the judge will throw the evidence out, and the criminal
                goes free.</p>

            <h3>What is it?</h3>
            <p>The Chain of Custody is a simple paper (or digital) trail that shows exactly who had the evidence at
                every second. It must answer these questions:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Who</strong> collected the device?</li>
                <li><strong>When</strong> did they collect it (exact time)?</li>
                <li><strong>Where</strong> was it stored?</li>
                <li><strong>Who</strong> had the keys to the evidence room?</li>
                <li><strong>When</strong> was it taken out for analysis?</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="pro-tip">
                <strong>Analogy:</strong> Imagine a relay race. The evidence is the baton. If one runner drops the baton
                or hands it to a stranger, the team is disqualified. In court, "disqualified" means the evidence is no
                longer "admissible."
            </div>

            <h3>The Consequences of Failure</h3>
            <p>Defense lawyers are experts at finding "breaks" in the chain. If an investigator takes a suspect's phone
                home for the weekend before putting it in the evidence locker, the lawyer will argue: "How do we know
                the investigator didn't add fake messages to the phone over the weekend?" Even if the investigator is
                honest, the <em>doubt</em> is enough to win the case for the criminal.</p>

            <p>In one famous case, a piece of digital evidence was rejected because the police officer who seized it
                didn't write down the serial number of the laptop. When they got to court, the officer couldn't prove
                that the laptop in the evidence bag was the same one he took from the suspect's house. The case was
                dismissed. Details matter.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 5: EVIDENCE HANDLING -->
        <section id="handling">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 05</span>
            <h2>Handling Evidence Like a Pro</h2>
            <p>Digital evidence is "volatile," which means it can change or disappear in an instant. Handling it
                requires special tools and extreme care.</p>

            <h3>Rule #1: Never work on the original</h3>
            <p>A forensic investigator NEVER looks at the original computer or phone directly. Why? Because simply
                opening a folder changes the metadata of that folder. Instead, they make a <strong>"Forensic
                    Image"</strong> (a perfect bit-for-bit clone) and work on the clone.</p>

            <h3>The Magic of Hashing</h3>
            <p>How do we prove the clone is exactly the same as the original? We use a "Digital Fingerprint" called a
                <strong>Hash</strong> (usually SHA-256). If even one single bit of data changes on the drive, the hash
                value will completely change. By showing the court that the hash of the original matches the hash of the
                clone, we prove the evidence hasn't been tampered with.</p>

            <div class="feature-grid">
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>🚫 Write Blockers</h4>
                    <p>These are physical devices that you plug between the computer and the evidence drive. They allow
                        data to be READ but physically stop any data from being WRITTEN. It's like a one-way street for
                        data.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>🛡️ Faraday Bags</h4>
                    <p>When you seize a phone, you immediately put it in a Faraday bag. These bags block all radio
                        signals (Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, 5G). This prevents the criminal from remotely "wiping" or deleting
                        the phone's data from a distance.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>The Order of Volatility</h3>
            <p>Not all data lasts the same amount of time. Investigators have to act fast based on the "Order of
                Volatility":</p>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>RAM (Memory):</strong> Disappears the moment power is lost. Contains passwords and open chat
                    windows.</li>
                <li><strong>Network Cache:</strong> Temporary data about internet connections.</li>
                <li><strong>Hard Drives:</strong> Semi-permanent data.</li>
                <li><strong>Backup Tapes/Cloud:</strong> Permanent data.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>If you find a computer that is turned ON, you should **never** turn it off until you have captured the
                RAM, or you will lose the most important evidence forever! This is called "Live Response."</p>

            <p>Imagine finding a computer that is currently logged into an encrypted dark-web marketplace. If you pull
                the plug, the computer will lock itself, and without the password, that evidence is gone forever. But if
                you capture the RAM while it's still running, you might find the decryption keys or even the marketplace
                password right there in the memory.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 6: TOOLS -->
        <section id="tools">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 06</span>
            <h2>The Tools of the Trade</h2>
            <p>While investigators are smart, they need powerful software to sift through billions of 1s and 0s. Here
                are the "superhero" tools of the forensic world:</p>

            <div class="feature-grid">
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>🔍 Autopsy</h4>
                    <p>A famous open-source tool. It's great for beginners and professionals alike. It can automatically
                        find web history, images, and deleted files on a hard drive and show them in an easy-to-read
                        list.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>🔓 Cellebrite</h4>
                    <p>The king of mobile forensics. It's a special device and software that can bypass passcodes on
                        many iPhones and Androids to get the data inside. It is used by almost every major police force
                        in the world.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>🧠 Volatility</h4>
                    <p>A tool specifically for looking at RAM. It can "freeze time" and show what programs were running
                        and what passwords were typed just before the investigator arrived.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="feature-card">
                    <h4>📡 Wireshark</h4>
                    <p>Used for network forensics. It lets you "see" the data packets flying through the air or over
                        cables. It's like having X-ray vision for the internet.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p>There are also specialized tools like **Magnet AXIOM**, which is designed to pull evidence from
                everywhere — the computer, the phone, and the cloud — and put it into one single timeline. This is
                incredibly helpful because it allows an investigator to say: "At 2:05 PM, he sent a text message from
                his phone; at 2:07 PM, he searched for 'how to hide a body' on his laptop; and at 2:10 PM, he uploaded a
                file to Dropbox."</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 7: LEGAL STANDARDS -->
        <section id="legal">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 07</span>
            <h2>The Courtroom Battle</h2>
            <p>Finding the evidence is only half the job. The other half is explaining it to a judge and a jury who
                might not even know how to use an iPad. This is where <strong>Legal Standards</strong> come in.</p>

            <h3>Admissibility</h3>
            <p>For evidence to be "admissible" (allowed in court), it must meet certain criteria:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Authentic:</strong> Can you prove it came from the suspect's computer and wasn't planted by
                    a hacker or a jealous ex?</li>
                <li><strong>Reliable:</strong> Did the tools you used work correctly? Have those tools been tested by
                    other experts?</li>
                <li><strong>Complete:</strong> Did you show the whole story? If you only show the "bad" emails but hide
                    the ones where the suspect says "I'm just joking," you are breaking the law.</li>
                <li><strong>Believable:</strong> Can you explain it in simple English? If you start talking about
                    "binary offset headers," the jury will fall asleep.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="pro-tip">
                <strong>The Daubert Standard:</strong> In the US, this is a rule that says forensic methods must be
                "scientifically valid." This means the method must have been tested by other scientists, published in
                journals, and have a known "error rate." You can't just make up your own way of finding data!
            </div>

            <p>In many countries, investigators must also have a **Search Warrant** before they can even touch a
                computer. If they look at a phone without a warrant, everything they find is "poisoned" and cannot be
                used in court, no matter how much it proves the person is guilty. This is known as the "Fruit of the
                Poisonous Tree" doctrine.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 8: CASE STUDIES -->
        <section id="case-studies">
            <span class="section-number">MODULE 08</span>
            <h2>Real-World Stories</h2>
            <p>To see why this all matters, let's look at two famous cases where digital forensics saved the day.</p>

            <div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: 30px; border-left: 5px solid var(--accent);">
                <h4>The BTK Killer (Dennis Rader)</h4>
                <p>For 30 years, a serial killer known as "BTK" (Bind, Torture, Kill) terrorized Kansas. He used to send
                    letters to the police to taunt them. In 2004, he sent a purple floppy disk. He asked the police, "If
                    I send this, can you track me?" The police lied and said "No." When they got the disk, forensic
                    experts looked at the "deleted" metadata on the disk. They found a hidden record that said the disk
                    was last saved by a man named "Dennis" at the "Christ Lutheran Church." The police looked up the
                    church, found Dennis Rader was the president, and arrested him. He is now in prison for life. His
                    own digital footprints caught him after 30 years.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-card" style="border-left: 5px solid var(--success);">
                <h4>The Silk Road Seizure</h4>
                <p>Ross Ulbricht ran a massive illegal website called the Silk Road. The FBI knew he was the boss, but
                    they needed his laptop while it was **unlocked** to get the encryption keys. They followed him to a
                    public library. While he was distracted, one agent "accidentally" started a fight nearby. When Ross
                    turned his head to look, another agent grabbed his laptop while it was still open. This "Live
                    Seizure" allowed the FBI to see everything before the encryption could lock the drive. They found
                    his journal, his server passwords, and millions of dollars in Bitcoin.</p>
            </div>

            <p>These cases show that no matter how smart a criminal thinks they are, they always leave something behind.
                Whether it's a hidden file, a piece of metadata, or a logged-in session, the truth is always there in
                the bits and bytes. Digital forensics is the key that unlocks that truth.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 9: QUIZ -->
        <section id="quiz" class="quiz-section">
            <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 50px;">
                <span class="section-number">FINAL CHALLENGE</span>
                <h2>Knowledge Check</h2>
                <p>Prove you've mastered the art of digital investigation. You must get 100% to complete the module.</p>
            </div>

            <div id="quiz-container">
                <!-- Question 1 -->
                <div class="question-box" id="q1">
                    <div class="question-text">1. Why do we use "Hashing" (like SHA-256) in forensics?</div>
                    <div class="option-list">
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">A. To make the files smaller
                            and easier to send.</div>
                        <div class="option-item correct" style="pointer-events: none;">B. To prove that the evidence
                            hasn't been changed.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">C. To hide the evidence from
                            the suspect's lawyer.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">D. To delete the evidence
                            after we are done.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="explanation visible expl-correct" id="exp-q1">✓ Correct! Hashing creates a unique digital fingerprint; if the data changes, the fingerprint changes, proving tampering.</div>
                </div>

                <!-- Question 2 -->
                <div class="question-box" id="q2">
                    <div class="question-text">2. What happens when you "delete" a file from a computer?</div>
                    <div class="option-list">
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">A. It is immediately burned
                            and erased from the disk.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">B. It is moved to a secret
                            folder on the internet.</div>
                        <div class="option-item correct" style="pointer-events: none;">C. The computer erases the
                            "shortcut" to it, but the data stays until it's overwritten.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">D. It turns into a virus that
                            attacks the investigator.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="explanation visible expl-correct" id="exp-q2">✓ Correct! Computers usually just mark the space as 'available' instead of erasing the data immediately.</div>
                </div>

                <!-- Question 3 -->
                <div class="question-box" id="q3">
                    <div class="question-text">3. What is a "Faraday Bag" used for?</div>
                    <div class="option-list">
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">A. To keep the computer cool
                            so it doesn't melt.</div>
                        <div class="option-item correct" style="pointer-events: none;">B. To block signals so a phone
                            can't be remotely wiped.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">C. To carry heavy hard drives
                            more easily.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">D. To store evidence in the
                            refrigerator.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="explanation visible expl-correct" id="exp-q3">✓ Correct! Faraday bags block all electromagnetic signals like Wi-Fi and 5G, preventing remote data destruction.</div>
                </div>

                <!-- Question 4 -->
                <div class="question-box" id="q4">
                    <div class="question-text">4. Which of these is the MOST volatile (disappears fastest)?</div>
                    <div class="option-list">
                        <div class="option-item correct" style="pointer-events: none;">A. RAM (Computer Memory)</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">B. A USB Thumb Drive</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">C. A printed document</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">D. An old Hard Drive in a
                            closet</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="explanation visible expl-correct" id="exp-q4">✓ Correct! RAM is 'volatile memory,' meaning it is completely erased as soon as the power is cut.</div>
                </div>

                <!-- Question 5 -->
                <div class="question-box" id="q5" style="border-bottom: none;">
                    <div class="question-text">5. What is the "Chain of Custody"?</div>
                    <div class="option-list">
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">A. A metal chain used to lock
                            the evidence room.</div>
                        <div class="option-item correct" style="pointer-events: none;">B. A record of everyone who
                            touched the evidence and when.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">C. A list of all the websites
                            the criminal visited.</div>
                        <div class="option-item" style="pointer-events: none;">D. The password needed to open
                            a locked phone.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="explanation visible expl-correct" id="exp-q5">✓ Correct! The Chain of Custody is a legal paper trail that proves the evidence was handled properly by authorized people.</div>
                </div>
            </div>

